{
  "moment": "the knowledge surface — a hit, a miss that asks the app for help, and the answer coming back",
  "source": "JOS-486 (design surface 5 + boundary verdict 5; not a plan-doc moment — the four numbered moments are 01-04)",
  "note": "THE COMMITTED CORPORA MOVE ENGINE-SIDE, and with them the one thing the engine cannot do: fetch. `knowledge.item` and `knowledge.mob` answer off `items.json` and `mobs.json` — 12 MB that leaves main's heap — joined with the local quest catalogs, your own loot history (read off the fold's own index, which is why a `dropsSeen` count can appear beside a wiki drop table without a second process knowing anything) and the era evidence each drop's ITEM page carries. WHAT A MISS LOOKS LIKE is the middle of this conversation and it is the whole of boundary verdict 5: the engine ships without a network stack, so a name no corpus holds still ANSWERS — `found: false` beside a record carrying every local association it could gather, because a missing wiki page does not unmake a posky quest use — and it announces the name once, connection-wide, with no id and no epoch. The app fetches (its queue, its 150 ms spacing, its Retry-After cooldown) and pushes the answer back with `knowledge.define`, which is NOT a full-set replace and says so in the schema: the set is the wiki. The same lookup afterwards is a hit, served out of the overlay, and no second miss is ever announced for that name. `knowledge.search` closes the loop a lookup cannot: a lookup needs the exact name and a person types four letters, so the ENGINE ranks (exact, then prefix, then contains) because the renderer never munges domain data.",
